}\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs20 P?S/8 Tiny File System Internal Directory Structures\par
\par
Last edit:\tab 14-Jun-2016\tab cjl\par
\par
Note: Information regarding other P?S/8 file structures such as the extended-length file directory and the system programs directory in the basic P?S/8 system, or the P?S/8 SHELL file structure can be obtained from other documents covering these structures. \par
\par
The basic P?S/8 file structure for user-created files is the Tiny File System or \i TFS\i0  directory.  The P?S/8 keyboard monitor [and embedded line editor] includes a variety of commands to edit text files stored in the TFS directory, as well as additional commands to manage files within the directory [regardless of format].  All supported functions can be accomplished without running any system programs; as such, this component of the P?S/8 operating system is a fast and responsive environment for primary management of all TFS-based user files.  For additional documentation of the P?S/8 keyboard monitor consult the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  document available\cf0\f1\fs24  \cf1\f0\fs20 separately.\par
\par
[Note: There are additional P?S/8 system programs available to perform certain tasks by alternate means; however, there are tradeoffs associated with using programs that are oriented around only certain aspects of file management without providing any means to accomplish other functions found in the keyboard monitor or in other utility programs.  Users familiar with the keyboard monitor environment can generally examine files and update the TFS directory to carry out file-related functions more quickly than running other utilities.  For example, the keyboard monitor CA\{talog\} command supports no particular options [other than the ability to change the default logical device unit which is a feature of most keyboard monitor commands], yet produces virtually instantaneous results.  For a comprehensive set of potential directory printout options, the \i DIRECT\{ory\}\i0  program can be used as required; for more complex directory management operations potentially addressing multiple files via a wild-card filter mechanism, the \i FILMAN \i0 [File Manager] program is available.  These and other utilities must be initiated from the keyboard monitor; the system must reload after each program is executed before any other operation can be accomplished.  Depending on hardware specifics, this method, while clearly offering many additional options, may require considerably longer time to accomplish general file and directory navigation tasks.]\par
\par
All TFS files are exactly 2048 words long stored in 16 consecutive logical blocks of 128 12-bit words each.  The P?S/8 keyboard monitor requires valid internal parameters within the TFS directory to allow proper operation; there are specific requirements for valid file name characters within file slots.\par
\par
TFS file names can be as long as six characters where each character is either a letter, number or certain specific non-alphanumeric characters such as \i\\\i0 , \i ^\i0 , \i [\i0 , and \i ]\i0 .  Some users prefer to organize a particular programming project by using file names suggestive of pseudo-sequence numbers such as PRG[1], PRG[2], PRG[3], PRG[4],\par
PRG[5], PRG[6].  [Note: All P?S/8 system programs can be passed a maximum of 17 files.  Specific programs may impose further file restrictions.  For example, P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  will generally require binary output files which somewhat limits the maximum practical number of input files; virtually all system programs allow some combined total of 17 input and output files.]\par
\par
The internal entry for any TFS file always occupies three adjacent 12-bit words on a three-word boundary in sequence within blocks 0015-0016.  Figure 1 illustrates a few file entries in a minimally-used directory.\par
\par
Starting with an empty directory, the files TCBIN, TCBOOT, FOO and BAR were entered, then FOO was deleted creating a free slot.\page [Note: A file slot not currently assigned due to deletion is referred to as a free slot.  Empty slots after the last occupied slot are referred to as available slots.  While there is no practical difference between the two, free slots are not the highest in the directory as the storage is laid out in linear order.  Whenever a file is deleted by a keyboard monitor operation, a check is made if the file slot is the highest slot used, in which case the entire entry is zeroed out and the entry becomes the first of all of the available slots at the end of the directory.  This generally prevents the CA\{talog\} command output from having trailing empty entries. However, there are perverse circumstances where a series of free slots may end the command output.  This can always be eliminated by using the EN\{ter] command to create dummy file entries and then deleting them last entry first as required.  However, in doing so, attempts at recovery from accidental deletion will be hampered as all trailing characters in the file name are removed.  Free slots are indicated by the value 7777 in the first of the three file name words; the other two are retained.  This may aid in recovery from accidental file deletion since there may be artifact characters to help identify the file.  Regardless of circumstances leading to the free or available directory slot, a new file name can always be entered and the corresponding file can be loaded to attempt recovery.  More information on file recovery and many other subjects is available in the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available as a separate document.]\par
\par
TCBIN is five characters, the trailing six bits in the name entry for TCBIN is 00.  TCBOOT is six characters; the three words are a complete file name.  Since FOO was deleted, the first two characters are replaced by the value 7777.  The rest of the entry still retains O followed by three sets of six bits set to 00.  BAR is six characters and active; three sets of six bits set to 00 fill the rest of the three word entry.\fs12\par
\fs20\par
