Figure 2: Dump of Block 0016 of P?S/8 Version 8Z for TC08.\par
\par
\fs20 In the example given, the number of slots allowed would be 2700-1000/20 or 74 files allowed.  [Note: All examples are stated in terms of octal values; 74 octal files can also be expressed as 60 decimal files.]\par
\par
Since each file entry is three words, 3x74 files or 264 words of the directory can be used to represent files for this device.  Since the directory is loaded into 07000 and onward, the first entry address disallowed would be 7264.  The value in word[175] is expressed in two's complement form which is 0514. Thus, if a TFS entry is attempted, it would be rejected if it was calculated as 7264 [or higher] within the directory address range.\par
\par
For larger devices, additional file slots are allowed, up to the maximum capacity of 124 [84 decimal] files occupying 2500 blocks.  Extremely spartan DECtape-based storage units could permit this much file usage by eliminating virtually all system programs.  As a practical example, TFS file storage can start at block 0200 [as of\par
P?S/8 Version 8Z] by eliminating all system programs.  The maximum capacity of the TFS directory will just fit on a standard DECtape of 2702 blocks; the files will be stored in blocks 0200-2677.  [Note: Setting up a DECtape for use as a TFS files-only auxiliary tape can easily be accomplished; however, there are presently no standard utilities to automatically implement such a reconfiguration of resources.  With a proper understanding of the TFS and system programs directory, this can easily be accomplished by an advanced user using either P?S/8 DUMP or P?S/8 BLKODT to implement the slight changes to the TFS and System Programs directories.  Future releases of P?S/8 will include reconfiguration utilities to allow all users the ability to adjust file allocation storage as required.]\line\line DECtapes are occasionally available with 3300 blocks; the maximum file capacity can be achieved by eliminating all system programs past block 0577.  As of Version 8Z, this allows retention of PAL, FOCAL and most other system programs.  Alternatively, the approach above could be used to allow additional storage beyond the TFS directory for use as the extended-length file on the device.  [Note: Extended-length file support is currently undergoing redesign; documentation will be provided for future releases when the implementation is stable.  The \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  includes interim descriptions of how to take advantage of this structure as of Version 8Z.]\page File block boundary considerations.\par
\par
While there are no inherent requirements for any blocks within a TFS directory to be on any particular boundary, P?S/8 execution commands require that all files be aligned to boundaries of eight blocks.  This restriction exists to allow P?S/8 to communicate the starting block and logical unit of any file in 12-bit words.  With this restriction in place, the block number can be expressed in the high-order nine bits of the 12-bit word while the logical unit can be expressed in the low-order three bits of the same 12-bit word.  Lists of such words are passed to system programs that will process the passed values accordingly.\par
\par
Files from earlier systems.\par
\par
Very early P?S/8 systems did not impose the boundary restriction stated above; the logical unit concept had not yet been implemented.  It is not likely any of these early systems exist presently; however, the forerunner system known as the R-L Monitor System [or MS/8] for TC01/TC08 DECtape was submitted to DECUS and in theory was used by certain individuals who are likely candidates for upgrade to P?S/8. \par
The R-L Monitor System imposes no boundary restrictions as logical units are not supported.  [The R-L Monitor System is for a single TU55 or TU56H [half] drive on a TC01 or TC08 only; no other configuration is supported.]\par
\par
The R-L Monitor System also uses the TFS directory format and is compatible with the P?S/8 Version 8Z keyboard monitor when used as a non-system logical device unit for file access [read purposes] only.  Files in binary format can be copied directly as the R-L Monitor binary file format is a compatible subset of that used for P?S/8 binary files [lacking support for extended memory].  Text files require conversion using the P?S/8 stand-alone \i RLFIX\i0  utility [available from the author of this document].\line\line It is recommended the R-L Monitor System tape be backed up before upgrading, as the RLFIX program modifies the supplied DECtape; all converted text files will no longer be compatible with the R-L Monitor System; the conversion process is not reversible.  The selected files will be upgraded to P?S/8 text format and stored back in-place with no loss of data.  After the upgrade process has completed, all selected text [and binary] files will be fully compatible with P?S/8 standards; however, the TFS directory will not be in compliance with the boundary requirement needed for full access by P?S/8 programs.\par
\par
As such, the files will require transfer to another P?S/8 device unit.  [Note: The R-L Monitor System only supports TC01/TC08 DECtape as does the RLFIX program; as such, the conversion must generally be completed using the TC01/TC08 release of\par
P?S/8 or perhaps a TD8E-based P?S/8 release.]  The file transfer can be accomplished with keyboard monitor commands such as FE[tch] filnam:1 followed by WR[ite] filnam:0 or similar operations.  Alternatively, the P?S/8 FILMAN utility can be used to allow files to be copied in groups using the wild-card match feature of FILMAN; file types can be restricted at the same time if desired.  [Additional information about P?S/8 FILMAN is available in other documents.]\par
\par
Once the files have been upgraded and transferred to boundary-conforming media, the files can be used with all relevant P?S/8 utilities.\par
\par
[Note: All users are encouraged to embellish text documents using horizontal tabs within the file.  Unfortunately, The R-L Monitor System does not support this character as valid within text.  As such, users should consider additional editing of the files after conversion.]\par
\par
